# What if you paint the light, not the thing? Monet's Impression, Sunrise
tags: ["impressionism","monet","light-and-color","painting-technique","art-history"]
thumb: "band \"Paint the light, not the thing\" picture \"File:Monet_-_Impression,_Sunrise.jpg\" colour"
teaches: ["art-history/impressionism-movement","concepts/light-as-subject","techniques/color-over-form","art-analysis/how-paintings-create-perception"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a warm, knowledgeable gallery guide, quietly amused","b":"gemini:Puck | a relaxed, good-humoured visitor with a dry, understated wit; curious, never exaggerated"}
prompt: "How little is actually drawn? How did this painting give a movement its name?"
next: {"title":"Why paint the same church thirty times? Monet's Rouen Cathedral","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/how-painters-caught-light/08-why-paint-the-same-church-thirty-times.cast"}

## Impression, Sunrise: light, not things
sources:
    - id: marmottan
      title: Impression, soleil levant (inv. 4014)
      authors: Musée Marmottan Monet, Paris
      year: 2024
      finding: 1872; Le Havre's outer harbour at dawn; shown at the 1874 exhibition in Nadar's former studio; Monet's 'Impression' title; Louis Leroy's review in Le Charivari, 25 April 1874
      url: https://www.marmottan.fr/en/notice/4014/
    - id: wiki
      title: Impression, Sunrise
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Olson and colleagues (2014) date it to 13 November 1872, about 7:35 a.m.; Leroy's wallpaper jibe; the sun has the same luminance as the sky
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impression,_Sunrise
    - id: livingstone
      title: 'Vision and Art: The Biology of Seeing'
      authors: Margaret Livingstone
      year: 2002
      finding: 'The sun is equiluminant with the sky: it vanishes in a black-and-white copy and seems to shimmer'

    image art cites ["marmottan","wiki"] credit "Claude Monet, 1872. Musée Marmottan Monet, Paris. Public domain." x 500 y 335 width 800 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Monet_-_Impression%2C_Sunrise.jpg/1920px-Monet_-_Impression%2C_Sunrise.jpg look screen regions.boat [0.425,0.59,0.1,0.165] regions.boats_behind [0.14,0.535,0.2,0.105] regions.cranes [0.64,0.31,0.3,0.12] regions.harbour [0,0.15,1,0.5] regions.masts [0.08,0.12,0.37,0.35] regions.reflection [0.565,0.515,0.075,0.405] regions.signature [0.04,0.925,0.21,0.045] regions.sky [0,0,1,0.3] regions.smoke [0.2,0.25,0.06,0.09] regions.sun [0.592,0.287,0.032,0.041] regions.sun_column [0.54,0.23,0.13,0.7] regions.sun_glow [0.55,0.235,0.115,0.135] regions.water [0.03,0.7,0.94,0.24] hidden true
    portrait monet of "Claude Monet" cameo true hidden true
    image bw cites ["livingstone"] credit "The same painting in black and white (Gutza, public domain)" x 500 y 335 width 800 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/82/Claude_Monet%2C_Impression%2C_soleil_levant%2C_1872_BW.jpg look screen view [0.005,0,0.99,1] reveal develop hidden true

    card title "Impression, Sunrise"

B: This is Monet's Impression, Sunrise. I see a sun and a lot of fog. Where's the harbour?
    draw art

A: Under the fog: the port of Le Havre, the town Monet grew up in, at dawn.
    camera on ["art:harbour"] zoom 1.15 duration 3

A: And that is this painting's question: what if you paint the light, and hardly the things at all?
    camera reset true duration 3

A: Astronomers have used the sun, the tides and the smoke to date it:
    highlight art:sun effect ring lift true

A: on their reckoning, a November morning in 1872, the day before Monet turned thirty-two.
    draw monet

    erase monet

B: Let me see what's actually there. Masts, cranes, a chimney smoking?
    blocking false

    pause 1.4

    point at.ref art:masts gesture arrow duration 1.1

    point at.ref art:cranes gesture arrow duration 1.1

    point at.ref art:smoke gesture arrow duration 1.1

A: All there, and barely drawn: a mast is one stroke, a crane a few lines, the smoke a blue smudge.
    blocking false

    pause 1.6

    point at.ref art:masts gesture arrow duration 1.4

    point at.ref art:cranes gesture arrow duration 1.4

    point at.ref art:smoke gesture arrow duration 1.6

    ask "What is the most solidly painted thing here? Tap it." spot boat on art voice a
        right: Yes: the little rowing boat in front, the most solid thing in the picture.
        wrong: Look for the darkest shape on the water.

    camera on ["art:boat"] zoom 3

A: And it's a few dark dabs, with a man standing to row.
    point at.ref art:boat gesture arrow

    camera reset true

B: So he just didn't finish it?
    pause 0.2

A: He finished what he was after.
    camera on ["art@[0.2,0.0,0.6,0.5]"] duration 2.5

A: The things are only hints; the subject is the colour of the morning air.
    camera reset true duration 3

B: Then at least the sun must be the brightest thing in it.
    highlight art:sun effect ring lift true

    quiz "Which is brighter: the sun, or the sky around it?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 300 buttons_at.y 560 voice a
        right: They match: measured for brightness, the sun is no brighter than the sky around it.
        wrong: Picture it with no colour at all.
        * The orange sun
        * The grey sky around it
        + Neither: they match

A: Take the colour  (@draw bw@)away, and the sun all but disappears.

    camera on ["art@[0.5,0.2,0.22,0.2]"]

B: It's gone! I can barely find where it was.
    pause 0.3

A: Only its colour holds it there.
    camera reset true

    erase bw

A: The neuroscientist Margaret Livingstone argues that's why it seems to shimmer: the eye can't quite fix where it is.
    blocking false

    pause 1.5

    highlight art:sun effect ring duration 2

    highlight art:sun effect pulse duration 2.5

A: So the light is carried by colour alone: one orange sun (@highlight art:sun art:reflection strength strong rest grey lift true@), and its broken reflection, a column of quick dashes on the water.

B: So how did a painting this sketchy name a whole movement?
    pause 0.2

A: In 1874, Monet and his friends put on their own show in Paris, in the photographer Nadar's old studio.
    pause 0.2

A: Asked for a title for the catalogue, Monet said it could hardly pass for a view of Le Havre, so: put Impression.
    highlight art:signature effect ring lift true

B: And the critics, I imagine, were charmed.
    pause 0.2

    camera on ["art:water"]

A: One, Louis Leroy, called his mocking review The Exhibition of the Impressionists, and joked that half-made wallpaper looked more finished than this seascape.
    pause 0.2

    camera reset true

A: The insult stuck: by their third show, in 1877, a friend of theirs was printing a little journal called The Impressionist.
    pause 0.2

B: So it's not really a picture of the harbour. More of the fog?
    camera on ["art:harbour"] zoom 1.1

    camera on ["art@[0.4,0.15,0.4,0.8]"]

A: Of the light in the fog: not ships and cranes, but how one low sun made them look, for a few minutes of one morning.
    highlight art:sun_column rest paper lift true

    camera reset true

    quiz "A photographer wants a foggy morning on a bridge the way Monet would paint it. Which follows his method?" voice a
        right: Soft shapes and one patch of colour: the light becomes the subject, and the bridge is only a hint.
        wrong: Which choice keeps the light, and lets the things go?
        * Crisp bridge, a hazy sky
        * Soft shapes, all in muted grey
        * Sharp focus on every bolt
        + Soft shapes, one bright colour

B: Turner's sunset told a story about a ship. Monet's sunrise is the story.
    highlight art:sun effect ring lift true

